{:up ["ALTER TABLE recipes ADD COLUMN view_count INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0"]
 ;; How often a Recipe was actually **consumed**: one increment per
 ;; `GET /api/recipes/:id?detail=full`, which is the only request in this API
 ;; that hands back the description of one Recipe. A listing carries no body at
 ;; any `?detail`, so scanning the shelf counts for nothing — the number says
 ;; somebody went and read *this* one. The UI's expand and an agent's fetch are
 ;; the same request, so one counter covers both without either client telling
 ;; the server what to count. See `recipe-handler/get-recipe-handler`, which is
 ;; the one place that writes it.
 ;;
 ;; **`DEFAULT 0` here is an epoch and not a claim that a row was never read.**
 ;; It is the third time this schema has had to say what a default means, and it
 ;; lands differently from the two before it. Recipes 1, 2 and 3 have all been
 ;; read, plenty of times, by the owner and by the agents that wrote them —
 ;; nothing counted, so nothing can be recovered. What makes 0 honest anyway is
 ;; that this column counts **reads recorded from this migration forward** and
 ;; every existing row starts from that same point: the number is a count since
 ;; the epoch, so no row is misattributed *relative to another* and the ranking
 ;; built on it (see `db.recipe/list-recipes`) compares like with like from day
 ;; one. That is exactly what 005 could not say — a `DEFAULT 'machine'` would
 ;; have pinned one specific actor on one specific version, a claim about a
 ;; particular past event rather than a shared starting line. 004's 0 was the
 ;; same kind of claim held back. This one is a scale with an origin, so the
 ;; mute default would be the misleading part: it counts from here, and the
 ;; docstrings say so where a reader meets the number.
 ;;
 ;; **On `recipes` and not on `recipe_history`**, for the reason `published` and
 ;; `tags` are not versioned either: a read is a fact about the Recipe and about
 ;; today, not about the version that happened to be current when it happened.
 ;; Nothing here can answer 'how often was v2 read' and nothing should pretend
 ;; to — the count is not archived by `archive!` and a save carries it forward
 ;; untouched, because the Recipe being read is the same Recipe afterwards.
 ;;
 ;; `NOT NULL DEFAULT 0` rather than nullable: unlike 005's third bucket there is
 ;; no 'unrecorded' category to preserve here. Every row's count is known and it
 ;; is a number, which also keeps the ranking arithmetic free of a null case —
 ;; `0.7 * NULL` is NULL, and a row that sorted as unknown would be a hole in an
 ;; ordering that has to be total.
 :down ["ALTER TABLE recipes DROP COLUMN view_count"]}
